WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.497
- Good morning Believe Nation!

00:00:01.497 --> 00:00:04.874
Today's message is do extraordinary.

00:00:04.874 --> 00:00:06.824
Over to you, Bear Grylls.

00:00:06.824 --> 00:00:08.765
(rooster cawing)

00:00:08.765 --> 00:00:11.370
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.369
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:14.369 --> 00:00:18.557
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.557 --> 00:00:19.390
- And it was something I was told

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.738
on the first day of ever
joining the Special Forces,

00:00:21.738 --> 00:00:23.393
and I remember at the end of this,

00:00:23.393 --> 00:00:25.689
long selection process,

00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:26.939
where 180 of us

00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:29.842
had been whittled down

00:00:29.842 --> 00:00:31.009
to four of us.

00:00:32.541 --> 00:00:35.562
And this guy who I'd never seen before,

00:00:35.562 --> 00:00:37.824
came in, stood in front of us,

00:00:37.824 --> 00:00:38.907
and said this

00:00:42.381 --> 00:00:44.592
"The difference between ordinary

00:00:44.592 --> 00:00:46.092
and extraordinary,

00:00:47.443 --> 00:00:50.610
is often just that little word, extra.

00:00:51.998 --> 00:00:54.933
And the work I'm going
to now ask you to do

00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:56.837
is not going to be ordinary,

00:00:56.837 --> 00:00:58.892
it's going to be extraordinary.

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:01.163
But what's going to make the difference,

00:01:01.163 --> 00:01:03.046
is when not one

00:01:03.046 --> 00:01:04.046
ounce of you

00:01:05.112 --> 00:01:07.097
thinks you can.

00:01:07.097 --> 00:01:10.727
You're the people who over this last year

00:01:10.727 --> 00:01:13.373
have shown me you can turn around,

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:16.206
and give me that little bit extra.

00:01:18.423 --> 00:01:19.590
And it's that,

00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:24.474
that's going to make your
work here extraordinary."

00:01:24.474 --> 00:01:25.503
- To me extraordinary is

00:01:25.503 --> 00:01:28.345
do you settle for good enough or not?

00:01:28.345 --> 00:01:31.045
I think generally in life,
we tend to settle for good.

00:01:31.045 --> 00:01:32.976
There's a book Good to
Great that talks about

00:01:32.976 --> 00:01:35.663
the enemy of great, is good.

00:01:35.663 --> 00:01:37.386
The enemy of great isn't terrible.

00:01:37.386 --> 00:01:38.599
Because if something is really terrible,

00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:39.649
you're going to fix it.

00:01:39.649 --> 00:01:41.057
But if something is good,

00:01:41.057 --> 00:01:43.092
then you let it slide,
you let it go through.

00:01:43.092 --> 00:01:44.528
You know it would be
better but you're like

00:01:44.528 --> 00:01:45.589
Eh, just it's good.

00:01:45.589 --> 00:01:46.422
It's good enough.

00:01:46.422 --> 00:01:47.255
Lets put it through.

00:01:47.255 --> 00:01:48.852
And that's how too many
people live their lives,

00:01:48.852 --> 00:01:50.358
they've settled for a good life,

00:01:50.358 --> 00:01:51.706
instead of going for a great one.

00:01:51.706 --> 00:01:53.454
Instead of going for an extraordinary one.

00:01:53.454 --> 00:01:55.653
And so, you can apply it
to the work you're doing,

00:01:55.653 --> 00:01:57.660
today you can apply it to
the videos you're making.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.613
You can apply it to your
relationship at home,

00:01:59.613 --> 00:02:01.142
with your wife, with your husband,

00:02:01.142 --> 00:02:03.537
with your kids, with your
friends, with your parents,

00:02:03.537 --> 00:02:05.120
with your customers you're
going to call tomorrow.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.743
You can apply it to
everything that you do.

00:02:06.743 --> 00:02:08.224
Do you settle for good?

00:02:08.224 --> 00:02:09.702
Or do you go that little bit extra,

00:02:09.702 --> 00:02:11.731
to make it extraordinary?

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:14.564
I think about the example, the
50 Cent video that we made,

00:02:14.564 --> 00:02:15.896
and I told this story a couple times

00:02:15.896 --> 00:02:17.675
but I think it really resinates here.

00:02:17.675 --> 00:02:19.630
Where my team spent a lot
of time making a video,

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:21.715
50 Cent's Top Rules for Success

00:02:21.715 --> 00:02:25.263
and I watched it, and it was
good, it was a good video.

00:02:25.263 --> 00:02:26.366
I liked it.

00:02:26.366 --> 00:02:28.351
But I felt it could be better.

00:02:28.351 --> 00:02:30.913
I felt 50 had more in him,
that we hadn't uncovered,

00:02:30.913 --> 00:02:32.433
that we should be able to find and share.

00:02:32.433 --> 00:02:35.393
So then I spent extra hours of my time,

00:02:35.393 --> 00:02:38.864
putting together the
research to make it happen.

00:02:38.864 --> 00:02:41.771
I was doing the job of somebody on my team

00:02:41.771 --> 00:02:43.657
because I thought it could be better.

00:02:43.657 --> 00:02:46.172
I didn't want to settle for
having just a good enough video.

00:02:46.172 --> 00:02:47.501
And so, it took a ton of man hours

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:48.759
to put this video together.

00:02:48.759 --> 00:02:49.690
And when we launched it,

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:50.523
it had success.

00:02:50.523 --> 00:02:52.415
And some how it go to 50 Cent.

00:02:52.415 --> 00:02:53.490
I don't know 50 Cent.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:54.397
But he saw it,

00:02:54.397 --> 00:02:55.388
he liked it,

00:02:55.388 --> 00:02:56.436
he shared it.

00:02:56.436 --> 00:02:57.886
He wrote a blog post about it,

00:02:57.886 --> 00:02:58.806
and tweeted it,

00:02:58.806 --> 00:03:00.583
and I think he put it on his Facebok.

00:03:00.583 --> 00:03:01.689
He shared it.

00:03:01.689 --> 00:03:04.153
Now would the first
video have gotten to him,

00:03:04.153 --> 00:03:04.986
and would he have shared it?

00:03:04.986 --> 00:03:06.546
Maybe.

00:03:06.546 --> 00:03:07.756
The one that was just good enough?

00:03:07.756 --> 00:03:09.030
Maybe.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.401
But he saw something extraordinary,

00:03:11.401 --> 00:03:12.847
that he loved,

00:03:12.847 --> 00:03:13.935
and he wanted to share it.

00:03:13.935 --> 00:03:15.538
And that often, is what
makes a difference.

00:03:15.538 --> 00:03:16.798
And you know,

00:03:16.798 --> 00:03:18.952
you know when the thing you
made was just good enough.

00:03:18.952 --> 00:03:20.657
You know when you're just skating by.

00:03:20.657 --> 00:03:22.722
And you know when you
put everything you had

00:03:22.722 --> 00:03:24.340
into making the next thing
you're doing amazing.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.607
I want you to be proud of
the work that you're doing.

00:03:26.607 --> 00:03:29.144
I want you to be able to show something,

00:03:29.144 --> 00:03:30.107
that you made,

00:03:30.107 --> 00:03:30.940
every day,

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.436
to want to give it to your grandkids.

00:03:32.436 --> 00:03:34.214
I want my grandkids to watch these videos,

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:36.569
and first say how young I looked.

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:37.498
And then say,

00:03:37.498 --> 00:03:40.567
"Man you really poured a lot
of energy and love into it,

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:41.937
we're proud of you."

00:03:41.937 --> 00:03:43.960
And I want them to be
inspired and learn from it.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.163
And it doesn't mean that,

00:03:45.163 --> 00:03:46.353
I'm not still going to get better.

00:03:46.353 --> 00:03:48.614
What's extraordinary for me now,

00:03:48.614 --> 00:03:50.430
won't be in a couple years.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.587
My best thing two years ago,

00:03:52.587 --> 00:03:54.762
pales in comparison to what I can do now.

00:03:54.762 --> 00:03:56.569
Because I've worked harder,

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:57.656
I've learned, I've built my muscles,

00:03:57.656 --> 00:03:58.867
I'm better.

00:03:58.867 --> 00:04:01.493
And I still strive to get
better, and better, and better.

00:04:01.493 --> 00:04:02.953
There's is no perfection.

00:04:02.953 --> 00:04:04.767
It's only something that we strive for.

00:04:04.767 --> 00:04:08.028
So to encourage you, to stop
settling for good enough.

00:04:08.028 --> 00:04:09.351
Look at what you've done today,

00:04:09.351 --> 00:04:10.625
depending on what time
you're watching this video.

00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:12.552
Look at what is on the schedule for today.

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:14.665
And don't go to sleep tonight,

00:04:14.665 --> 00:04:18.640
until you can say you did
something that was extraordinary.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:19.598
So the question today is,

00:04:19.598 --> 00:04:20.601
I'm curious.

00:04:20.601 --> 00:04:22.424
What have you done today
that's extraordinary?

00:04:22.424 --> 00:04:24.894
Or what are you going to
do that's extraordinary?

00:04:24.894 --> 00:04:28.710
Where you will not settle
for just being good enough.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:29.870
Leave it down on the comments below.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:31.557
Super curious to find
out what you have to say.

00:04:31.557 --> 00:04:33.349
Also, I want to give a quick
should out to William Young.

00:04:33.349 --> 00:04:35.336
William thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:04:35.336 --> 00:04:36.708
of my book Your One Word.

00:04:36.708 --> 00:04:38.306
It really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:04:38.306 --> 00:04:40.339
And I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:04:40.339 --> 00:04:41.477
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:04:41.477 --> 00:04:42.600
I believe in you.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.461
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself!

00:04:44.461 --> 00:04:45.982
And whatever your one word is!

00:04:45.982 --> 00:04:47.096
Much love,

00:04:47.096 --> 00:04:48.617
and I'll see you guys
again tomorrow morning

00:04:48.617 --> 00:04:51.339
for another shot of an espresso.

00:04:51.339 --> 00:04:53.380
♫ I wake up every morning

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:57.380
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.537
(typewriter typing)

00:04:59.537 --> 00:05:00.678
- You know,

00:05:00.678 --> 00:05:02.030
when you're chasing a dream,

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:03.550
I mean, when you're working hard,

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:05.646
chasing something you love,

00:05:05.646 --> 00:05:06.479
and,

00:05:07.676 --> 00:05:08.509
um,

00:05:09.576 --> 00:05:10.687
completely dedicated.

00:05:10.687 --> 00:05:12.222
It just happens.

00:05:12.222 --> 00:05:14.696
You don't, you can't really notice it.

00:05:14.696 --> 00:05:16.061
Even still to this day,

00:05:16.061 --> 00:05:19.228
I'm in an amazing position in my life.

00:05:20.767 --> 00:05:22.826
But I'm still working

00:05:22.826 --> 00:05:25.856
like I'm not in this position.

00:05:25.856 --> 00:05:26.689
- [Interviewer] Absolutely.

00:05:26.689 --> 00:05:28.806
- I'm still working like none of this,

00:05:28.806 --> 00:05:29.639
you know what I mean?

00:05:29.639 --> 00:05:30.472
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:30.472 --> 00:05:31.634
- It's just something I love to do,

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:32.598
I just keep pushing, I keep working hard.

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:36.098
I can't pin point an exact moment for you,

00:05:36.984 --> 00:05:39.947
if anything I always felt
like it was happening

00:05:39.947 --> 00:05:41.147
way back then.

00:05:41.147 --> 00:05:42.818
And now it is happening.

00:05:42.818 --> 00:05:47.123
But now I almost feel
like it's not happening.

00:05:47.123 --> 00:05:48.294
I want to push to something else.

00:05:48.294 --> 00:05:49.254
- [Interviewer] Absolutely.

00:05:49.254 --> 00:05:50.609
- I'm always striving to push forward.

00:05:50.609 --> 00:05:51.929
- The idea of being super rich,

00:05:51.929 --> 00:05:53.618
the idea of operating from abundance,

00:05:53.618 --> 00:05:55.276
the idea of going out and
making the world your prey.

00:05:55.276 --> 00:05:57.118
The idea of building something

00:05:57.118 --> 00:05:58.689
that no one else can believe in.

00:05:58.689 --> 00:06:00.460
You can believe in.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.163
God gives you this vision.

00:06:02.163 --> 00:06:04.075
He gave it to you, and you alone.

00:06:04.075 --> 00:06:07.413
You have to carry a
cross, for a little bit.

00:06:07.413 --> 00:06:08.654
On your own.

00:06:08.654 --> 00:06:09.675
People will watch you.

00:06:09.675 --> 00:06:11.510
First they'll cheer.

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:12.607
Then they'll be upset with you,

00:06:12.607 --> 00:06:13.818
Then they'll get real mad.

00:06:13.818 --> 00:06:14.943
You'll be on their couch

00:06:14.943 --> 00:06:17.195
and you get off their couch,

00:06:17.195 --> 00:06:19.731
and you'll be this hero.

00:06:19.731 --> 00:06:21.096
It's impossible,

00:06:21.096 --> 00:06:22.977
if God gave you that vision

00:06:22.977 --> 00:06:24.289
for you to fail at it.

00:06:24.289 --> 00:06:26.299
Unless you quit.

00:06:26.299 --> 00:06:29.031
So that's a very important lesson.

00:06:29.031 --> 00:06:30.121
Because it seems like

00:06:30.121 --> 00:06:30.984
in every business,

00:06:30.984 --> 00:06:33.433
everybody quits all the time.

00:06:33.433 --> 00:06:34.378
The success stories

00:06:34.378 --> 00:06:36.164
are the ones that went the extra mile.

00:06:36.164 --> 00:06:39.114
- You can be asking for assignments

00:06:39.114 --> 00:06:41.601
that are above your level.

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:43.521
So there's work that you do at your level,

00:06:43.521 --> 00:06:46.154
and there's work you do above your level.

00:06:46.154 --> 00:06:47.243
And the only way you're

00:06:47.243 --> 00:06:50.824
going to be promoted to do
work above your level is,

00:06:50.824 --> 00:06:52.889
a, if you can demonstrate
that you're capable

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:54.591
of doing the work at your level.

00:06:54.591 --> 00:06:58.256
And b, if you are proactive in seeking out

00:06:58.256 --> 00:07:01.362
opportunities that expand your capability.

00:07:01.362 --> 00:07:03.490
And give you a chance to work

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:05.343
above the level that you're at.

00:07:05.343 --> 00:07:06.693
Nothing is going to happen

00:07:06.693 --> 00:07:08.888
unless you take that initial action.

00:07:08.888 --> 00:07:10.853
Nothing's going to happen.

00:07:10.853 --> 00:07:11.686
Nothing's going to change.

00:07:11.686 --> 00:07:13.837
Unless you put down that
pen, in the meeting,

00:07:13.837 --> 00:07:14.670
and you sit up,

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:15.844
and you pay attention.

00:07:15.844 --> 00:07:18.846
And you listen for your
opportunity to speak.

00:07:18.846 --> 00:07:20.592
Nothing's going to happen.

00:07:20.592 --> 00:07:22.611
Unless you push yourself to go in,

00:07:22.611 --> 00:07:25.245
and make that ask for that assignment

00:07:25.245 --> 00:07:27.189
that scares you to death.

